WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.270
Film Courage: Do you think a screenwriter
should write their first draft as quickly

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:07.930
as possible, just regurgitate it out of themselves
and then revise from there?

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:08.930
Pilar Alessandra, Writer and Instructor: Ahh!

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.070
You’re talking about the vomit draft aren’t
you, right?

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.460
We have such nice expressions for it.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.520
Yes, I do.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.760
And I urge my students not to look back.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.430
That’s where the perfections start to come
in and they stop up their writing process

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.110
and they have these beautiful first acts and
then they never go any further.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.410
So yes, by all means, write to the end.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:37.699
And then you can go back in and you can start
honing in or chiseling away at it, fattening

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:39.100
it up, making it better.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.770
But write to the end.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.870
Film Courage: So if they plan to do a 90-page
script, don’t stop in the middle and make

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:46.870
sure that it’s okay.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.500
They’ve got to just get it all out.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.850
Pilar Alessandra: Right, barrel forward.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:56.420
I think so many people make the mistake of
going back and rereading what they wrote the

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.719
day before and polishing up with what they
wrote and only moving this much further.

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:02.940
We don’t have enough time, you know?

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.770
We only set so much writing time a day.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:09.020
You’ve got to make as much use of that as
you can and press forward.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.280
Film Courage: Is there a timeframe for writing
this first script?

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.920
Pilar Alessandra: I think that timeframe is
personal.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.659
It really depends on where you can fit it
in your life.

00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:23.430
You know, I’ve made my whole philosophy
of teaching is writing in ten minute increments

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:29.289
because being a busy person myself and knowing
that my writers are busy, so I do believe

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:33.509
that what ever time you can make the best
use of it as long as you focus.

